---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import SiteNav from "../components/SiteNav.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../components/SiteFooter.astro";
/* /why (B3/B4). Mission-first essay with the Codornices creek story in
   the back half (on paper, set apart). Locked copy-why.md; claims-clean
   (Wildcat Creek explicitly hypothetical, $5,000 personal, Reticulum
   credited). Photo slot = C4 (the real first two nodes). Awaits Conor's
   B6 voice pass + the personal origin sign-off (received). */
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
const repo = "#"; // A7
---
<Base title="Why we're building Mycelium" description="Good ground truth is priced like a luxury, so the small places decline in the dark. Why we built Mycelium: free, open telemetry so they get watched too.">
  <SiteNav current="why" />

  <header class="page-head" id="main">
    <div class="wrap narrow">
      <p class="kicker">Why.</p>
      <h1>Why we're building Mycelium.</h1>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="essay">
    <div class="wrap narrow">
      <h2>The world is changing faster than we're measuring it.</h2>
      <p>Ask a simple question about almost any small piece of the natural world
      — is this creek warming? when did the oxygen drop? what did last summer
      actually do to this meadow? — and the honest answer is usually: nobody
      knows. Not because nobody cares, but because nobody was measuring.</p>
      <p>Continuous environmental monitoring exists, and it works. It's also
      priced like laboratory equipment: a single commercial telemetry station
      runs thousands of dollars before the cellular or satellite subscription
      starts, and the data often lands in a platform you rent rather than own.
      At those prices, monitoring goes where grants and mandates send it — a few
      important rivers, a few flagship reserves — and the rest of the living
      world goes unwatched. Satellites fill in some of the picture, but they see
      broad, not deep: a satellite can tell you a region is drying; it cannot
      tell you what happened in the pool below the culvert on Tuesday night.</p>
      <p>The places that get degraded first are exactly the places too small,
      too local, too unglamorous to monitor. They decline in the dark.</p>

      <h2>What we're doing about it.</h2>
      <p>Mycelium is a free and open environmental telemetry mesh, the flagship
      project of LBM Labs. The complete design is published: hardware parts list,
      firmware, software, assembly guides. You build a node from a few hundred
      dollars of off-the-shelf parts, and your readings land in a spreadsheet or
      endpoint you own, with no subscription anywhere in the stack.</p>
      <p>Three design decisions carry most of the weight:</p>
      <p><strong>Every node is a relay.</strong> Nodes find each other
      automatically over license-free LoRa radio and carry each other's traffic,
      so coverage compounds: each sensor anyone deploys also extends the network
      for everyone behind it. That's how readings are meant to get out of the
      valleys and canyons where cellular has never reached and satellite uplinks
      were previously the only option. As a hypothetical: to monitor water
      temperature along the East Bay's Wildcat Creek, a single gateway with WiFi
      at the Tilden visitor center could connect a chain of nodes running up the
      watershed — much of it without cell coverage — at zero ongoing cost.</p>
      <p><strong>Nobody has to trust anybody.</strong> Every reading is encrypted
      end to end on the sensor node and decrypted only at its owner's
      destination. Relays and gateways carry ciphertext they cannot read — so
      strangers' networks can strengthen each other without sharing anything but
      radio waves.</p>
      <p><strong>It assumes it will be neglected.</strong> Field telemetry fails
      in boring ways: power cuts, wedged software, dead radios, silent months.
      Every reading is committed to durable storage the moment it's taken and
      deleted only after its destination cryptographically confirms receipt;
      nodes watchdog themselves back to life; and the failure drills that prove
      this ship with the code, so you can re-run the evidence instead of taking
      our word.</p>
      <p>The network stack underneath is <a href="https://reticulum.network">Reticulum&nbsp;↗</a>,
      Mark Qvist's phenomenal open mesh networking project — Mycelium exists in
      large part because his work made it imaginable.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="creek" data-theme="paper">
    <div class="wrap narrow">
      <h2>The creek this started with.</h2>
      <p>Growing up in west Berkeley, one of my favorite places was the 150 feet
      or so of Codornices Creek daylighted along the Ohlone Greenway near Gilman
      Street. It is far from wild — it runs under the BART tracks and into
      concrete tunnels at both ends. But on warm days after preschool my
      godfather would take me there to "fish" with a piece of string and a bent
      nail baited with cheese. I never caught anything. I remember trout nibbling
      at the cheese while crayfish picked along the bottom and hid in the undercut
      banks, and I remember that this scrap of urban nature felt magical.</p>

      <figure class="photo-slot" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="ph-lbl mono">photo</span>
        <span class="ph-desc">the real first two nodes — a leaf and a gateway</span>
      </figure>

      <p>The trout are gone now. Plastic bags drift where they used to hold. The
      occasional crayfish still works the bottom, looking less like a member of an
      ecosystem than a survivor of one. The same quiet degradation is repeating
      the world over, in uncountable small fragments of nature exactly like this
      one.</p>
      <p>Here is the part that bothers me most, despite living nearby my whole life:</p>
      <blockquote class="thesis">I could not tell you when it happened.</blockquote>
      <p>Warmer water or a diesel spill; a decade or a single bad weekend — I
      don't know, because nobody was measuring. And nobody was ever going to spend
      $5,000 on a telemetered data logger for 150 feet of urban creek.</p>
      <p>Mycelium is the tool I wished existed: telemetry cheap enough, reliable
      enough, and simple enough that places like this get watched too.</p>
      <p class="close"><em>I'd like to know what's happening to my creek while the
      crayfish are still there to ask about.</em></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="stands">
    <div class="wrap narrow">
      <h2>Where it stands, honestly.</h2>
      <p>Mycelium is young. A complete network — sensor node, mesh, gateway,
      encrypted pipeline to a spreadsheet — runs end to end on the bench today,
      with its reliability layer tested by scripted failure drills against the
      live system. Field hardware (solar power, enclosures, real-world radio
      range) is still being proven, and we won't publish performance claims until
      it has been. The roadmap, like everything else, is public.</p>
      <p>If you're an academic, a watershed group, an agency, a teacher, or just
      someone with a creek — we're building this for you, and it will never cost
      more than the parts.</p>
      <p class="dim">The story of what happens next is partly yours to write.</p>
      <div class="ctas">
        <a class="btn-primary" href={`${base}/build`}>Build one</a>
        <a class="btn-ghost" href={repo}>Read the code</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SiteFooter />
</Base>

<style>
  .page-head { padding: var(--s-9) 8vw var(--s-4); }
  .essay, .creek, .stands { padding-block: var(--s-7); }
  .essay p, .creek p, .stands p { font-size: var(--t-body); line-height: 1.7; max-width: 64ch; }
  .essay h2, .creek h2, .stands h2 { margin-top: var(--s-7); }
  .essay h2:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
  .essay strong { color: var(--accent); }
  [data-theme="paper"] .essay strong { color: var(--accent-deep); }

  .thesis {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 650;
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3.4vw, 2.4rem);
    line-height: 1.15;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    color: var(--ink-dark);
    margin: var(--s-6) 0;
    padding-left: var(--s-5);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-deep);
    max-width: 20ch;
  }
  .creek .close { color: var(--dim-dark); }
  .creek .photo-slot {
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9; margin: var(--s-6) 0; border: 1px dashed color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent-deep) 35%, transparent);
    border-radius: var(--r-sm); display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center;
    justify-content: center; gap: var(--s-2); text-align: center;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent-deep) 4%, transparent);
  }
  .creek .ph-lbl { font-size: 11px; letter-spacing: 0.12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent-deep); }
  .creek .ph-desc { font-size: var(--t-small); color: var(--dim-dark); }
  .stands .ctas { margin-top: var(--s-5); }
</style>
